{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>\"And he said to them: I am one hundred and twenty years old today\"</b> — this is to comfort them concerning his matter, meaning: I am old and you no longer have benefit from me, and moreover HaShem has commanded me that I shall not cross there, so do not tremble and do not fear, for YHWH will cross with you; He will not remove His Shekhinah from you on my account, and Joshua is the one who crosses before you in my place. And even though Moses our master was in his strength and his health, as Scripture testified (Deuteronomy 34:7), \"his eye had not dimmed and his vigor had not fled,\" he said this to them to comfort them. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 31:2) that \"I can no longer go out and come in\" is because HaShem said to me, \"You shall not cross the Jordan.\" And this is not correct. And according to the opinion of R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 31:2), \"go out and come in\" refers to going out and coming in for battle, because his strength had weakened in his old age. This too is not correct. And our Sages said (Sotah 13b) that this teaches that the wellsprings of wisdom had been stopped up from him, and this was by way of a miracle, so that he would not be distressed at conferring greatness upon Joshua in his presence."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Talmud citation 'סוטה יג' refers to Sotah 13b, the standard folio reference for this passage; the leaf designation is abbreviated in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:57:07.608131672Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}